// The higher-cells flagship's instance: the category of discrete setoids,
// written as a module whose law fields are typed members.
//
// An object is a type, a hom is a function, and equality is `Path`. Naming the
// discreteness, because it is the part a later reader would otherwise have to
// rediscover: a discrete setoid's equivalence IS `Path`, so "respects the
// relation" is congruence and every function satisfies it. A setoid with a
// CHOSEN equivalence needs `El : Setoid -> Type`, a large elimination the
// surface has no path to, and that is why this instance is the discrete one.
//
// WHAT THIS WITNESSES. The operations and both unit laws check, and the law
// endpoints name the module's OWN operations -- `comp(p, p, q, id(p), f)`,
// not a private helper. That distinction is the whole point: a law about a
// helper is a different theorem under the same name, and the staged design
// says a rule's endpoint composite is definable precisely because the
// operations are named fields.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT WITNESS. The module carries no `Model(CatShape)` ascription,
// because that signature cannot be written: its operation clause binds every
// free sort variable as a dependent parameter, and the surface type grammar has
// no dependent function type (gandr-3jus). So this checks the instance, and not
// the instance against the stated signature. Associativity is absent for a
// second reason. Bind associativity now reaches the normal form, and the law
// converges when its composites are written pointwise over `compose` -- but
// that spelling names a private helper, and a law about a helper is a different
// theorem under the same name. In the model-faithful spelling the composites
// nest one operation's application inside another's argument at thunk type, one
// level deeper than the embedding resolution reaches (gandr-rson).
//
// The type variables are named `t u v` and `p q` rather than `a b c`. Type
// substitution renames a capturing type binder apart (gandr-ijdw), so the
// design's own spelling is admissible here; the spelling is retained because
// restoring it witnesses nothing in THIS program, measured by ablation rather
// than assumed. The walker is live over this file -- renaming a law member
// turns it red -- and a corpus witness written for the capture path does
// separate the two sides. These two spellings of this module do not, which is
// a fact about the program and not about the surface.

module SetoidCat {
  def ident(t : Type, x : t) -> F t { ret x }

  def compose(t : Type, u : Type, v : Type, f : U[ω] (t -> F u), g : U[ω] (u -> F v), x : t) -> F v {
    run y <- f(x);
    g(y)
  }

  def id(t : Type) -> F (U[ω] (t -> F t)) { ret thunk { ident(t) } }

  def comp(t : Type, u : Type, v : Type, f : U[ω] (t -> F u), g : U[ω] (u -> F v)) -> F (U[ω] (t -> F v)) {
    ret thunk { compose(t, u, v, f, g) }
  }

  def unitL(p : Type, q : Type, f : U[ω] (p -> F q)) -> F Path((U[ω] (p -> F q)), comp(p, p, q, id(p), f), f) {
    ret here(f)
  }

  def unitR(p : Type, q : Type, f : U[ω] (p -> F q)) -> F Path((U[ω] (p -> F q)), comp(p, q, q, f, id(q)), f) {
    ret here(f)
  }

}

// The harness checks. The two law fields are present as MEMBERS, which is the
// claim the corpus could not previously state, and neither elaborated at a type
// mentioning the gradual unknown -- a law accepted at such a type is inhabited
// trivially rather than proved.
//@ expect: clean
//@ expect-member: SetoidCat.id
//@ expect-member: SetoidCat.comp
//@ expect-member: SetoidCat.unitL
//@ expect-member: SetoidCat.unitR
//@ expect-member-type-without-unknown: SetoidCat.id
//@ expect-member-type-without-unknown: SetoidCat.comp
//@ expect-member-type-without-unknown: SetoidCat.unitL
//@ expect-member-type-without-unknown: SetoidCat.unitR
